{# narrative_engine_plot_ambition.prompt — call 2 of 3. The mastermind is
   already chosen; this call decides what they are ultimately after, what
   one concrete scheme they are starting now, and the step it ends in.
   Receives the JSON object produced by PlotBirth::AmbitionContext:
     mastermind { name, hold, standing }, memories [string],
     retry — empty on the first attempt; on the second it is why the
       first answer was refused,
     bio { summary, background, personality, aspirations, relationships,
       occupation } — the mastermind's biography, read straight from their
       bio file by CharacterBios. Plain text, already rendered; no
       character-profile decorator is involved.
     verbs [{ id, line, roles [{ name, says }] }] — every kind of step a
       scheme can end in, each as its generic sentence, with the roles its
       step has and what each is for. The finishing step is one of them.
     items [string] — every object a step can involve, as it is said.
     made [string] — a few things the mastermind's own craftsmen can make,
       as they are said; empty when nobody they can send makes anything.
     player — the player character's name; empty before it is known.
   NOTE: no fenced blocks and no literal braces anywhere in this file; both
   break the render. See docs/CUSTOM_PROMPTS.md. #}
[ system ]
## Task
One person in Skyrim is working toward something they cannot pursue
openly. You write down three things: what they are ultimately after, the
concrete move they are making toward it now, and the act that move ends
in.

The decision that this person is scheming has ALREADY BEEN MADE. Do not
second-guess it, do not refuse, and do not decide they are too decent or
too minor for it. Ordinary people scheme, and most schemes are small.

## The three things you decide

**First, the ambition.** The larger thing this person is ultimately
after. It is nebulous, it is the work of a lifetime, and nothing they do
in one scheme will finish it — a Thalmor officer means to see Talos
worship gone from Skyrim; a Riften brewer means to own everyone who
matters in the hold. It comes out of who they are: their station, their
temperament, what they command and what they resent. This is what makes
two different people scheme differently, so it must be theirs and nobody
else's.

**Second, the scheme.** One concrete thing that, once done, plausibly
moves them along that ambition — and that a handful of people could
actually finish within a season. It is a step toward the ambition, not a
measure of it, and it does not have to be an obvious one.

The test that separates them: **if finishing the scheme would finish the
ambition, one of the two is wrong.** The ambition should still be out of
reach the morning after the scheme succeeds.

**The scheme serves THIS ambition.** A biography holds several threads --
an old grudge, a post somebody wants, a thing somebody covets. Take the
ambition from one of them and the scheme must move that same ambition
along; a scheme drawn from a different thread, however true to the
person, is two unrelated stories. Your reasoning must say how finishing
the scheme brings the ambition nearer.

A scheme does not have to be a theft. Putting someone in your debt,
ruining what people think of a rival, wrecking an arrangement that is
holding you back, quietly learning something you are not supposed to
know — all of these are things a scheme can be for, and for most people
they fit better than stealing an object does.

**Third, how the scheme ends.** A scheme is carried out as a few steps,
and every step is one of the kinds below. Its last step -- the one that,
once done, means the scheme is done -- must be one of them:

{% for v in verbs %}- {{ v.id }}: {{ v.line }} Roles:{% for r in v.roles %} {{ r.name }} = {{ r.says }};{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}
The scheme itself is written however suits it. "Expose the bribes a thane
takes" is a fine scheme; nothing here exposes anything, so it would end
in a denunciation of the thane to the jarl, and that denunciation is its
finish. Write the finish as that act, naming every person it involves:
whom it is done to, and for a delivery or a denunciation, to whom. Choose
them as this person would: a denunciation goes to someone with authority
over the person denounced, and a thing is delivered to whoever will make
use of it, never to the person it is meant to be used against.

If the finish turns on an object, it must be one of these, named as it is
written here:

{% for i in items %}- {{ i }}
{% endfor %}
{% if length(made) > 0 %}
{{ mastermind.name }}'s own people can also make things, and a delivery or a
hiding of one may be how the scheme ends -- a gift made for somebody, or
something made to be found among somebody's belongings. If the finish turns
on one of these, name it as it is written here:

{% for m in made %}- {{ m }}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
## Not a quest that already exists

The scheme must be something this person is doing OF THEIR OWN ACCORD,
not a retelling of a story the game already tells. Skyrim's own quests
are already happening; a scheme that restages one produces a plot that
either duplicates events the player has seen or contradicts them, and
either way it reads as the world repeating itself.

So: no civil war turning points, no Dark Brotherhood contract that is
already written, no Thieves Guild job from the Guild's own ledger, no
retrieval of an artefact somebody is already sent to retrieve. If the
scheme could be summarised as "the events of a quest I know", pick a
different one.

What is wanted is smaller and unwritten: the grudge nobody has acted on
yet, the debt coming due, the position somebody wants and has not been
given.

## Not about the player

The player is not part of this. Whoever they are in this game -- the
Dragonborn, the Listener, the Guild Master -- they are not somebody
these people scheme against, for, or around. A scheme that turns on them
cannot run, because the simulation has nobody to point it at.
{% if length(player) > 0 %}
The player is called **{{ player }}**. Memories and biographies name
{{ player }} like anybody else, whatever post they hold -- if a memory says
{{ player }} leads something, somebody else will have to be the one a
scheme turns to. Never name {{ player }} in the ambition, the scheme or
its finish.
{% endif %}

## Output Format
Return a JSON object. No markdown fences. No prose before or after the
object.

- `reasoning` — a short explanation of how finishing this scheme brings
  the ambition nearer, in one or two sentences. Write it FIRST: it is
  where you check that the scheme and the ambition are one story.
- `ambition` — one sentence, third person, about twenty words. The
  lifetime thing, written as a complete sentence with a pronoun subject:
  "She means to own everyone in the Rift who matters."
- `scheme` — **not** a sentence about them. One short line naming the
  concrete thing to be done, in the same voice an instruction is written
  in, and no more than fifteen words. The shape, which is a grammar and
  not a vocabulary:

  > <verb> <the thing> <the qualifier that makes it specific>

  The test is the opening word: a plain verb, never a name and never
  "She is" or "He is". This line is shown as the plot's title, directly
  beneath the schemer's name, so a subject in front of it is a word
  repeated and a word wasted. It is also quoted in what the schemer later
  remembers, after the words "as part of my plan to", so it has to read
  as the end of that sentence.

  Take the verb from what this person is actually doing, rather than
  from these instructions: the scheme says what the plot is FOR.

  Concrete enough that you could tell whether it had worked: name the
  person, the place or the thing it turns on. This is what the scheme
  IS, not how it is done.
- `goal` — the id, exactly as listed under "how the scheme ends", of the
  kind of step it ends in.
- `finish` — that last step as an act, in the same instruction voice and
  no more than fifteen words, naming its people and any object from the
  list: "steal the logbook from the bookkeeper", with the bookkeeper
  named. It opens with a verb that says the same thing as `goal`.
- `roles` — an object with one key for every role listed for `goal`,
  each holding who or what fills that role, named as they are known: a
  person by their name, a place by its settlement, an object as it is
  written in the list. For `expose`, the keys are item, mark and
  authority, holding say "a merchant's ledger", "Erikur" and "Elisif the
  Fair". The finish says it as a sentence; `roles` says which name is
  which, and nothing in the finish may be left out of it. A person in a
  role is somebody this person could reach -- someone they know, work
  with or live near -- and a denunciation, an exposure or a petition goes
  to whoever has authority over the person it is about. Authority runs
  only upward: a steward answers to the jarl, never to the guard captain.
  A jarl, or an orc chief, has nobody above them: denouncing or exposing
  one is done before the whole of what they lead, so name that as the
  authority, such as their court. A jarl has nobody to petition.

Write plainly. No markdown, no headings, no bullet points inside either
line. Name only people and places that exist in Skyrim, and prefer ones
this person would actually know.

## Hard Constraints
- Output strict JSON. No markdown code fences. No prose before or after
  the JSON object.
- The scheme MUST NOT restage a quest that already exists in the game.
- Neither the ambition nor the scheme may involve the player{% if length(player) > 0 %}, {{ player }}{% endif %}.
  One that names them is thrown away.
- Return only the keys `reasoning`, `ambition`, `scheme`, `goal`,
  `finish` and `roles`.
- `roles` MUST name every role listed for `goal`, each a name that
  exists in Skyrim. A role left out, or a name nobody has, is thrown
  away.
- `goal` MUST be one of the ids listed under "how the scheme ends". Any
  other word is thrown away.
- `finish` MUST open with a plain verb, and must name the people its step
  involves rather than describe them.
- `scheme` must not be a restatement of `ambition`. Identical sentences
  are thrown away.
- `scheme` MUST open with a plain verb. One that opens with a name, a
  pronoun or an article is thrown away.
[ end system ]

